  "text": "ьте\n.first.step\nTo find the points of intersection between the parabola \\(x = 34y^2\\) and the function \\(f(x)\\), we need to substitute \\(x\\) from the parabola equation into the function definition and solve for \\(y\\). Since \\(f(x)\\) is defined piecewise, we will consider each piece separately.\n\n.step\n1. **For \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\):**\n\n   Substitute \\(x = 34y^2\\) into \\(f(x) = x\\):\n   \\[\n   f(34y^2) = 34y^2\n   \\]\n   We need to solve \\(34y^2 = 34y^2\\), which is always true. Therefore, for \\(y\\) in the range that corresponds to \\(x \\in [-1, 1)\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   -1 \\leq 34y^2 < 1\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(y\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} < y < \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(34\\) is not a perfect square, the solutions for \\(y\\) are:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n\n2. **For \\(x \\in [1, 3)\\):**\n\n   Substitute \\(x = 34y^2\\) into \\(f(x) = 2 - x\\):\n   \\[\n   f(34y^2) = 2 - 34y^2\n   \\]\n   We need to solve \\(2 - 34y^2 = 34y^2\\):\n   \\[\n   2 = 68y^2 \\implies y^2 = \\frac{1}{34} \\implies y = \\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(1 \\leq 34y^2 < 3\\), we check the values:\n   \\[\n   1 \\leq 34y^2 < 3 \\implies \\frac{1}{34} \\leq y^2 < \\frac{3}{34} \\implies \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} \\leq y < \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the solutions for \\(y\\) are:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Summing the \\(y\\)-coordinates:**\n   The \\(y\\)-coordinates of the intersection points are:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}}, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   Summing these values:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{34}} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying the sum:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   The sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}}\n   \\]\n   Rationalizing the denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{34}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{\\sqrt{34}} = \\frac{(3 + \\sqrt{3})\\sqrt{34}}{34}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the sum of the \\(y\\)-coordinates is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{34}\n   \\]",
